msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/krylov-quantum-diagonalization/extracted-outputs/e3e0d1f3-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/krylov-quantum-diagonalization/extracted-outputs/e3e0d1f3-1.avif)\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Appendix: The Hamiltonian-function (spectral-filter) viewpoint {#appendix-the-hamiltonian-function-spectral-filter-viewpoint}"
msgstr "Appendix: The Hamiltonian-function (spectral-filter) viewpoint {#appendix-the-hamiltonian-function-spectral-filter-viewpoint}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The main workflow presented KQD operationally: build a Krylov basis from real-time-evolved states, estimate the projected matrices $\\mathcal{H}$ and $\\mathcal{S}$, and solve the GEVP.\n"
"This appendix revisits the *same* computation from a complementary angle that explains **why** KQD works: the *Hamiltonian-function*, or *spectral-filter*, viewpoint [\\[3\\]](#references), [\\[5\\]](#references).\n"
"It reuses the 12-qubit model, time step $\\Delta t$, and the Krylov solution already obtained above; no new circuit execution is required.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ipinakita ng pangunahing workflow ang KQD nang operational: bumuo ng Krylov basis mula sa real-time-evolved states, tantiyahin ang projected matrices na $\\mathcal{H}$ at $\\mathcal{S}$, at lutasin ang GEVP.\n"
"Binibisita muli ng appendix na ito ang *parehong* computation mula sa isang complementary na anggulo na nagpapaliwanag kung **bakit** gumagana ang KQD: ang pananaw na *Hamiltonian-function*, o *spectral-filter* [\\[3\\]](#references), [\\[5\\]](#references).\n"
"Muling ginagamit nito ang 12-qubit na modelo, time step $\\Delta t$, at ang solusyon sa Krylov na nakuha na sa itaas; walang kinakailangang bagong execution ng circuit.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The reference state as an energy distribution {#the-reference-state-as-an-energy-distribution}"
msgstr "The reference state as an energy distribution {#the-reference-state-as-an-energy-distribution}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let the Hamiltonian have the eigendecomposition\n"
msgstr "Hayaang ang Hamiltonian ay may eigendecomposition na\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"H=\\sum_{m} E_m\\,|E_m\\rangle\\!\\langle E_m|,\n"
"\\qquad E_0\\le E_1\\le\\cdots,\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"H=\\sum_{m} E_m\\,|E_m\\rangle\\!\\langle E_m|,\n"
"\\qquad E_0\\le E_1\\le\\cdots,\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"with energy eigenstates $|E_m\\rangle$.\n"
"Any reference state can be expanded in this eigenbasis,\n"
msgstr ""
"na may energy eigenstates na $|E_m\\rangle$.\n"
"Anumang reference state ay maaaring i-expand sa eigenbasis na ito,\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"|\\psi_{0}\\rangle=\\sum_m a_m\\,|E_m\\rangle,\n"
"\\qquad a_m=\\langle E_m|\\psi_{0}\\rangle,\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"|\\psi_{0}\\rangle=\\sum_m a_m\\,|E_m\\rangle,\n"
"\\qquad a_m=\\langle E_m|\\psi_{0}\\rangle,\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"so it carries a **spectral weight** $p_m=|a_m|^2$ at each energy $E_m$.\n"
"The reference energy is the mean of this distribution, $\\langle H\\rangle_{0}=\\sum_m p_m E_m$.\n"
msgstr ""
"kaya't nagdadala ito ng **spectral weight** na $p_m=|a_m|^2$ sa bawat energy na $E_m$.\n"
"Ang reference energy ay ang mean ng distribution na ito, $\\langle H\\rangle_{0}=\\sum_m p_m E_m$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The eigendecomposition of a general $n$-qubit Hamiltonian is exponentially costly, so this picture is only a diagnostic, which is *not* part of the algorithm.\n"
"Here, however, we can compute it cheaply for the *same* $n=12$ problem: the Heisenberg Hamiltonian conserves the total excitation number, and the reference $|000001000000\\rangle$ carries a single excitation, so its entire spectral content lives in the single-excitation subspace whose dimension grows only linearly with $n$.\n"
"We therefore reuse the exact single-excitation block (already used above as a benchmark) and read off the reference distribution $\\{(E_m, p_m)\\}$ inside that subspace.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang eigendecomposition ng isang pangkalahatang $n$-qubit Hamiltonian ay exponentially mahal, kaya't ang larawang ito ay diagnostic lamang, na *hindi* bahagi ng algorithm.\n"
"Dito, gayunpaman, magagawa nating i-compute ito nang mura para sa *parehong* $n=12$ problema: pinapanatili ng Heisenberg Hamiltonian ang kabuuang bilang ng excitation, at ang reference na $|000001000000\\rangle$ ay nagdadala ng iisang excitation, kaya't buong spectral content nito ay nasa single-excitation subspace na ang dimension ay lumalaki lamang nang linear kasama ng $n$.\n"
"Muli nating ginagamit ang eksaktong single-excitation block (nagamit na bilang benchmark sa itaas) at kinukuha ang reference distribution na $\\{(E_m, p_m)\\}$ sa loob ng subspace na iyon.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQD learns a filter that reshapes this distribution {#kqd-learns-a-filter-that-reshapes-this-distribution}"
msgstr "KQD learns a filter that reshapes this distribution {#kqd-learns-a-filter-that-reshapes-this-distribution}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A **Hamiltonian function** $f(H)$ is defined through spectral calculus,\n"
msgstr "Ang **Hamiltonian function** na $f(H)$ ay tinutukoy sa pamamagitan ng spectral calculus,\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"f(H)=\\sum_m f(E_m)\\,|E_m\\rangle\\!\\langle E_m|,\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"f(H)=\\sum_m f(E_m)\\,|E_m\\rangle\\!\\langle E_m|,\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"or in other words, a weighted sum of eigenprojectors.\n"
"Applying it to the reference reshapes each spectral amplitude, $a_m \\to a_m f(E_m)$:\n"
msgstr ""
"o sa madaling salita, isang weighted sum ng eigenprojectors.\n"
"Ang paglalapat nito sa reference ay nagbabago ng anyo ng bawat spectral amplitude, $a_m \\to a_m f(E_m)$:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"f(H)\\,|\\psi_{0}\\rangle=\\sum_m a_m\\,f(E_m)\\,|E_m\\rangle .\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"f(H)\\,|\\psi_{0}\\rangle=\\sum_m a_m\\,f(E_m)\\,|E_m\\rangle .\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If $f$ were sharply peaked at the lowest energy ($f(E_0)=1$ and $f(E_m)\\approx 0$ otherwise) then $f(H)$ would act as a ground-state projector, and the normalized output would be (close to) the ground state.\n"
"Therefore, a good low-pass **spectral filter** in energy is exactly what we want.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kung matalim na peaked ang $f$ sa pinakamababang energy ($f(E_0)=1$ at $f(E_m)\\approx 0$ sa iba pang kaso) kung gayon ang $f(H)$ ay kikilos bilang ground-state projector, at ang normalized na output ay magiging (malapit sa) ground state.\n"
"Samakatuwid, ang isang magandang low-pass **spectral filter** sa energy ang eksaktong kailangan natin.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "KQD does not prescribe $f$ in advance. Instead it expands the filter in the real-time-evolution basis,\n"
msgstr "Hindi tinutukoy ng KQD ang $f$ nang maaga. Sa halip, ini-expand nito ang filter sa real-time-evolution basis,\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"f_{\\rm KQD}(E)=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{r-1} c_\\ell\\, e^{-i\\ell\\Delta t E},\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"f_{\\rm KQD}(E)=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{r-1} c_\\ell\\, e^{-i\\ell\\Delta t E},\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"a *trigonometric* function of the energy whose coefficients $\\{c_\\ell\\}$ are precisely the GEVP eigenvector solved for above.\n"
"Minimizing the Rayleigh quotient $\\mathbf{c}^\\dagger\\mathcal{H}\\mathbf{c}/\\mathbf{c}^\\dagger\\mathcal{S}\\mathbf{c}$ is therefore the same as *learning* the filter that best suppresses the excited-state weight of the reference.\n"
"Larger Krylov dimension $r$ gives the filter more degrees of freedom and a sharper peak at the ground-state energy.\n"
msgstr ""
"isang *trigonometric* na function ng enerhiya kung saan ang mga coefficient nitong $\\{c_\\ell\\}$ ay eksaktong ang GEVP eigenvector na na-solve para sa itaas.\n"
"Ang pag-minimize ng Rayleigh quotient $\\mathbf{c}^\\dagger\\mathcal{H}\\mathbf{c}/\\mathbf{c}^\\dagger\\mathcal{S}\\mathbf{c}$ ay katumbas din ng *pag-aaral* ng filter na pinakamahusay na sumusupress sa excited-state weight ng reference.\n"
"Mas malaking Krylov dimension $r$ ay nagbibigay sa filter ng mas maraming degrees of freedom at mas matalim na peak sa ground-state energy.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The helper below evaluates this learned filter on an energy axis; we then apply it to the reference distribution obtained above.\n"
msgstr "Sinusuri ng helper sa ibaba ang natutunang filter na ito sa isang energy axis; ilalapat natin ito pagkatapos sa reference distribution na nakuha sa itaas.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Visualize the filter and its flexibility {#visualize-the-filter-and-its-flexibility}"
msgstr "Visualize the filter and its flexibility {#visualize-the-filter-and-its-flexibility}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We first show the learned filter at the full Krylov dimension used above, then track how it sharpens as the dimension $r$ grows.\n"
msgstr "Ipapakita muna natin ang natutunang filter sa buong Krylov dimension na ginamit sa itaas, pagkatapos ay susubaybayan kung paano ito humahasa habang lumalaki ang dimension $r$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The bars show the reference spectral weights $p_m$ (before) and the filtered weights $p_m|f_{\\rm KQD}(E_m)|^2$ (after), together with the learned filter intensity $|f_{\\rm KQD}(E)|^2$ on a continuous energy axis.\n"
"The filter concentrates the weight onto the lowest energy of the single-excitation subspace &mdash; the same energy that the KQD estimate converged to in the small-scale example.\n"
"Note that this is the ground state *within the single-excitation sector*, which is the relevant target for this excitation-conserving reference state, not the global ground state.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ipinapakita ng mga bar ang reference spectral weights $p_m$ (bago) at ang filtered weights $p_m|f_{\\rm KQD}(E_m)|^2$ (pagkatapos), kasama ang natutunang filter intensity $|f_{\\rm KQD}(E)|^2$ sa isang tuloy-tuloy na energy axis.\n"
"Inikonsentra ng filter ang weight sa pinakamababang enerhiya ng single-excitation subspace &mdash; ang parehong enerhiya na kinombergehan ng KQD estimate sa small-scale example.\n"
"Tandaan na ito ay ang ground state *sa loob ng single-excitation sector*, na siyang kaugnay na target para sa excitation-conserving reference state na ito, hindi ang global ground state.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/krylov-quantum-diagonalization/extracted-outputs/app-code-plot-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/krylov-quantum-diagonalization/extracted-outputs/app-code-plot-0.avif)\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Increasing the Krylov dimension: flexibility of the learned function {#increasing-the-krylov-dimension-flexibility-of-the-learned-function}"
msgstr "Increasing the Krylov dimension: flexibility of the learned function {#increasing-the-krylov-dimension-flexibility-of-the-learned-function}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Recall that the learned filter is a trigonometric polynomial in the energy with $r$ coefficients,\n"
msgstr "Tandaan na ang natutunang filter ay isang trigonometric polynomial sa enerhiya na may $r$ coefficients,\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"f_{\\rm KQD}(E)=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{r-1} c_\\ell\\, e^{-i\\ell\\Delta t E}.\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{equation*}\n"
"f_{\\rm KQD}(E)=\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{r-1} c_\\ell\\, e^{-i\\ell\\Delta t E}.\n"
"\\end{equation*}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Krylov dimension $r$ is exactly the number of free coefficients, so it controls the **flexibility** of the function.\n"
"A small $r$ can only produce a broad, gently varying filter that leaks weight into low-lying excited states; when $r$ increases, the filter can form a narrower peak at the target energy and suppress the remaining excited-state weight more aggressively.\n"
"This is the spectral-filter counterpart of the energy convergence observed in the small-scale example: as $r$ grows, the filtered distribution collapses onto the subspace ground state and the estimated energy decreases toward it.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang Krylov dimension $r$ ay eksaktong ang bilang ng free coefficients, kaya kontrolado nito ang **flexibility** ng function.\n"
"Ang maliit na $r$ ay makakagawa lamang ng malawak, mahinahong nagbabagong filter na tumutulo ang weight papunta sa mababang excited states; kapag tumaas ang $r$, maaaring bumuo ang filter ng mas makitid na peak sa target energy at supresahin nang mas agresibo ang natitirang excited-state weight.\n"
"Ito ang spectral-filter counterpart ng energy convergence na naobserbahan sa small-scale example: habang lumalaki ang $r$, ang filtered distribution ay kumukolapsa papunta sa subspace ground state at ang estimated energy ay bumababa patungo dito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We reuse the projected matrices already estimated above and simply solve the GEVP at each leading $r\\times r$ block, then evaluate and plot the corresponding filter.\n"
msgstr "Muli nating gagamitin ang mga projected matrix na na-estimate na sa itaas at simpleng lulutasin ang GEVP sa bawat leading $r\\times r$ block, pagkatapos ay susuriin at ipa-plot ang katumbas na filter.\n"

